{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The partitions of the Girvan–Newman method (Girvan and Newman 2002), coarsest first: repeatedly"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"remove an edge of greatest betweenness, and list the components each time their number grows."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"kind":"attribute","text":"@frozen"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"GirvanNewmanSequence"},{"kind":"text","text":"<"},{"kind":"genericParameter","text":"G"},{"kind":"text","text":"> "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"where"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"G"},{"kind":"text","text":" : "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Graph"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","anchor":"overview","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Returned by "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"girvanNewmanCommunities()"},{"type":"text","text":". Iterating it does the work, one split at a time: the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"first partition has one more community than the graph has components, each later one splits"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"one community in two, and the last is every vertex alone. A graph with no edges other than"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loops lists its components once. Each partition keeps a copy-on-write copy of the graph,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"as "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Partition"},{"type":"text","text":" does."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"CommunityDetection"}],"symbolKind":"struct","title":"GirvanNewmanSequence","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier